          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Mark Twain</strong><br>\nMark Twain, born Samuel Langhorne Clemens in 1835, grew up in Hannibal, Missouri. This small town along the Mississippi River influenced many of his famous works. At age 12, Twain lost his father and left school to become a printer's apprentice. Through this job, he discovered his love for writing as he helped publish articles for the newspaper.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Becoming a River Pilot</strong><br>\nTwain's dream was to become a steamboat pilot. He spent over two years learning the craft on the Mississippi River before earning his pilot's license. The name \"Mark Twain\" is actually a river term that means two fathoms deep, signaling safe water for steamboats.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Wild West</strong><br>\nIn 1861, Twain traveled to Nevada with his brother Orion. The rough and rowdy life of the Wild West sparked Twain's imagination. He worked at a variety of jobs, including mining for silver and gold, before turning to journalism full time.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
