          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Literary Breakthrough</strong><br>\nTwain's first major success came with the short story \"The Celebrated Jumping Frog of Calaveras County,\" which was published in 1865. The story's blend of humor and frontier realism won him national acclaim and led to a successful career as a writer and public speaker.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Adventures of Tom Sawyer'</strong><br>\nThe Adventures of Tom Sawyer, published in 1876, was one of Twain's best-known books. Based on his own childhood experiences, it tells the story of a boy growing up along the Mississippi River. The novel was popular for its characters, humor, and themes of freedom and adventure.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Adventures of Huckleberry Finn'</strong><br>\nConsidered by many to be Twain's masterpiece, \"Adventures of Huckleberry Finn\" was published in 1884. The book follows the journey of Huck Finn and a runaway slave named Jim as they travel down the Mississippi River. It addresses serious themes like slavery and racism, and is renowned for its vivid depiction of people and places along the river.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
