          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Twain's Later Years</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Twain faced personal tragedy and financial difficulties. Despite the death of his wife and two of his children, as well as bankruptcy, he continued to write and lecture. His later works included pointed social criticism and were darker in tone.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mark Twain's Legacy</strong><br>\nTwain is remembered as a great American writer and humorist. His works, which captured the American spirit, are still read and loved today. He was also known for his sharp wit and incisive satire, earning him the title of the \"greatest American humorist of his age.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Honors</strong><br>\nMark Twain died on April 21, 1910. He received many honors during his lifetime, including honorary degrees from Yale and Oxford universities. After his death, he was praised as a major figure in American literature, with his books being translated into various languages and enjoyed by readers around the world.</p>\n"
              },
